<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- A2 (ADR-0009). Lets HealthKit wake BADAN via HKObserverQuery when the types it reads
	     change, so the on-device repository is reasonably fresh without the user opening the app.
	     This is an entitlement, not a UIBackgroundModes key: HealthKit background delivery is
	     gated *here* and needs no background mode at all (plan §13.6).

	     BUG-BG-01 — read the next paragraph before acting on the one above. This comment used to
	     end with "there is deliberately no `UIBackgroundModes` entry", stated as a property of
	     the app rather than of HealthKit. That was true of HealthKit background delivery and
	     false of BGTaskScheduler, which the same change set added, and the two are gated by
	     different mechanisms:
	       • HealthKit background delivery — entitlement-gated (this file). No background mode.
	       • BGTaskScheduler              — background-mode-gated. `submit(_:)` throws
	         `BGTaskSchedulerError.unavailable` without UIBackgroundModes `fetch` (for
	         BGAppRefreshTask) and `processing` (for BGProcessingTask). The entitlement does
	         nothing for it and BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers only covers `register`.
	     Both modes are now declared in `project.yml`. Conflating the two mechanisms is what let
	     the app ship where no BG task could ever run, so scope any future claim about
	     UIBackgroundModes to the specific capability it describes.

	     What this does NOT change: an observer wake carries no payload and grants no new read
	     access. It only says "something changed"; the anchored re-query that follows reads exactly
	     the types NSHealthShareUsageDescription already names. No data leaves the device as a
	     result, which is why PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy is unchanged by A2.

	     Binding constraint from ADR-0009: the observer completion handler must be called within
	     seconds of every wake regardless of outcome. HealthKit permanently disables background
	     delivery for the app after 3 missed handlers, and there is no way to detect or reverse
	     that from inside the app — so any code that awaits ingestion before completing the
	     handler breaks this entitlement's value silently and for good. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit.background-delivery</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- EPIC-33 (Phase 3D). Shared container between BADAN and the BadanWidgets extension for the
	     bounded AmbientSnapshotDto only (today's ring score, one-sentence narrative, meds-due list,
	     last-night sleep summary — never a database, never raw health values). WidgetKit extensions
	     cannot open the SQLCipher store directly (extension memory limits, key-availability-before-
	     first-unlock, cross-process locking), so this is the ONLY channel between the app and its
	     widgets. Both BADAN.entitlements and BadanWidgets.entitlements must declare the identical
	     group id or the extension silently reads an empty/nonexistent container instead of failing
	     loudly — there is no runtime error for a mismatched group id, only missing data. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.net.binjamshid.badan</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
